## Ranked surviving finite decisions

1. **Garrity–Osterman, TRIP word complexity.** Literal endpoint: for every S(e,23,e)-adic language L in the source definitions and every n>=1, p_L(n)<=3n; the five conjugates and six twins are the same family. [2410.02032v2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.02032v2), Conjecture1 p31; July2026 revision. Exact identifier and selected object aliases have no hit in the 1,589-record qualified history screen; that is bounded evidence, not all-history clearance. Closest actual source is Section10, Propositions26–28 pp39–40, read with its extension tables and the proof/antecedent mechanism of the preceding section. Double-Gauss substitutions preserve a terminal delimiter; the extension graph can be updated from a finite boundary signature. A vertex with three left and three right extensions can create FOUR bispecial descendants, so the naive two-descendant induction is false. **Decisive calculation:** enumerate the signed bilateral multiplicities of these four descendants together, including their lengths, and prove a compensating order between positive and negative contributions. A sufficient quantified lemma is p_L(n+1)-p_L(n)<=3 for every admissible directive sequence and every n>=1. It is stronger than the source bound, not asserted equivalent. **Objection:** source itself already supplies this route; a table or a finite substitution sweep earns nothing, and the all-directive/all-length cancellation is still missing. No proof claimed.

2. **Fasi–Higham diagonal reciprocal Padé positivity.** Exact surviving question: for every odd m>=1 and every n>=0, [z^n](sum_(j=0)^m (-1)^j binom(m,j)(2m-j)!/(2m)! z^j)^(-1)>0. [Scardino2610.39051v1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39051v1), pp2–3, explicitly retains diagonal k=m after refuting unrestricted k,m. No exact ID/Fasi/Higham hit in the qualified history. Closest actual body: Theorem8.5 and its recurrence counterexamples, plus the diagonal caveat in Sections1–2; those examples have k!=m and do not refute this question. **Mechanism:** reverse-Bessel denominator recurrence, or a positive renewal representation for reciprocal coefficients. **Decisive calculation:** derive an induction coupling the diagonal orders m and m+2 that absorbs the negative even-j recurrence terms, uniformly in coefficient index n. **Objection:** fixed-m eventual pole dominance is insufficient for ALL n and is not uniform in diagonal m. This is an explicitly surviving subcase of ONE Padé family, not a fresh family for each m. Original Fasi–Higham publisher proof body not obtained, so no major computation or novelty claim.

3. **Herrman–van Hintum broadcast stabilization.** Exact endpoint: for every integer r>=2, there exists integer t0 such that for every integer t>=t0, delta_(t+1,r+2)(Z²)=delta_(t,r)(Z²), with graph l1 distance and infimal tower density. [1912.11560v1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.11560v1), Conjecture8 p8. No exact ID/broadcast-domination phrase hit in the qualified history. Closest actual body: Theorem1 and its discharging/excess proof for r=3,t>17; Lemma9 computes the excess of the known lattice, rather than a lower bound for arbitrary placements. **Mechanism:** assign each close tower-pair a bounded excess charge and charge defects to disjoint neighborhoods. **Decisive calculation:** a local charging lemma, valid for each fixed r and arbitrary tower set once t>C(r), reaching the lattice excess. **Objection:** r-uniform local cases proliferate, and the source already records failed casework; optimality is not a consequence of the construction. The continuous clause is the same family, not a second survivor.

